    "slug": "puntland-parliament-questions-education-ministry-on-performance-and-plans",
    "summary": "Puntland's parliament today questioned the Ministry of Education and Higher Education about its performance and plans, amid growing scrutiny of the education sector in the region.",
    "content": "The Puntland Parliament on [date] summoned officials from the Ministry of Education and Higher Education to answer questions regarding the ministry's recent activities and strategic plans. The session, held at the parliamentary building in Garowe, saw lawmakers press the ministry's leadership on its achievements and future initiatives.\n\nThe questioning comes amid heightened attention to the quality and accessibility of education in Puntland. In recent months, concerns have been raised by civil society groups and parents about teacher shortages, outdated curricula, and insufficient funding for public schools. The ministry has previously announced reforms aimed at improving enrollment rates and vocational training, but implementation has been slow.\n\nThe Minister of Education and Higher Education, [Name], led the ministry's delegation and faced queries from MPs representing various regions of Puntland. Key topics included the ministry's budget execution, the distribution of educational resources, and the status of ongoing school construction projects. Some legislators also raised questions about the ministry's coordination with international donors and NGOs operating in the education sector.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, developments in Puntland's education system are of particular interest. Many diaspora members fund schools and scholarships in the region, and the quality of education affects decisions about investing in local institutions. Additionally, diaspora families often rely on Puntland's schools and universities for their children's education when they return or maintain ties to the region. The parliamentary scrutiny signals a push for greater accountability, which could enhance donor confidence and diaspora investment.\n\nThe session concluded with the parliamentary committee on education requesting the ministry to submit a detailed report on its performance within two weeks. The committee will then review the report and may recommend further action. The ministry has not yet publicly responded to the questions raised.\n\nRead the original report from Horseed Media here: https://horseedmedia.net/barlamaanka-puntland-oo-suaalo-waydiiyey-madaxda-wasaaradda-waxbarashada/427167/",
